<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wu wei</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as non-action</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wu wei (無為) is a concept of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-philosophy">Chinese philosophy</a> that literally means not-acting or non-doing, and is variously translated as actionlessness, inaction or effortless action. In Daoism it describes the way the Tao acts, silently and without force, and Daoists aspire to live in alignment with it. The idea appears as early as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spring-and-autumn-period">Spring and Autumn period</a> and also figures in the Confucian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/analects">Analects</a> and in Legalist statecraft, where it names a ruler who leaves details to his ministers. Scholars such as Herrlee G. Creel distinguished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhuangzi">Zhuangzi</a>'s wu wei, a detachment from human affairs, from that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daodejing">Tao Te Ching</a>, a technique for governing.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Edward Slingerland believes the idea of wu wei predated the term, dating it back to the era of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/classic-of-poetry">Classic of Poetry</a>. Apart from the fragments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shen-buhai">Shen Buhai</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/analects">Analects of Confucius</a> are the only preserved text before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhuangzi">Zhuangzi</a> that uses the term directly. In one saying, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> asks whether Shun did nothing and yet ruled well, and answers that he merely corrected his person and took his proper position as ruler, facing south. Roger T. Ames regards attempts to determine the origin of wu wei as strained speculation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>For Slingerland, wu wei in the Confucian sense is the skill of becoming a fully realised human being, attained only by not consciously trying. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mencius">Mencius</a> held that people are already good and need only allow virtue to realise itself, while Xun Kuang considered wu wei possible only through long training. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laozi">Laozi</a> advocates a return to the primordial Mother and urges the reader to become like uncarved wood. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanzi">Guanzi</a> describes breath meditation and posture as ways of reaching it. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zen">Chan Buddhism</a> wuwei kept its sense of non-action, and Sengzhao wrote that for the sage action and non-action are identical.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shen-buhai">Shen Buhai</a> used the term for a ruler who, relying on administrative method, should not interfere with the duties of his ministers; he should hide his intentions and keep his own counsel. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shen-dao">Shen Dao</a> argued along the same lines, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-fei">Han Fei</a>'s enlightened ruler strikes terror into his ministers by doing nothing. Early Han emperors such as Emperor Jing were steeped in a Daoist laissez-faire, and the Huainanzi defines wu wei as meaning that no personal prejudice interferes with the universal Tao. Alan Watts described it as not-forcing, and Ursula K. Le Guin as doing without doing: trustful accomplishment, power that is not force.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wu wei” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Drawing on the Daoist idea of <b>wu wei</b>, Han Fei pictured a ruler who withdrew from active management, set universal standards and kept the “two handles” of reward and punishment in his own hands: “The enlightened ruler governs his officials; he does not govern the people.”</blockquote><a href="/ideology/legalism">Legalism · Core Ideas</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Wu wei</b>, literally &quot;non-action&quot;, is a central idea, with meanings ranging from &quot;not forcing&quot; to &quot;acting spontaneously&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/work/tao-te-ching">Tao Te Ching · What It Is</a></li></ul></section>
